this is a production of cornell  university  all right hi everybody my name is  savannah shellnutt i'm a second year  master's student in larry smartz program  and really my work over the past  on evaluating different hemp production  systems for new york state so  first year i looked at high density  plantings direct seed and high density  plantings last year i was involved with  a project sponsored by the new york farm  viability institute and we were  evaluating uh  the economic production differences  between high tunnels the outdoors and  greenhouses and during that trial we  noticed that there was an elevated  incidence of powdery mildew in our  tunnels and in order to make  high tunnels a really easily manageable  system in new york state we decided to  come up with a little fungicide  trial to determine what works best for  us so my advisor larry  knows somebody from utah state and he  stated that they thought that applying  velocinite which is a silicon crystal to  the soil might enable us to easily  control powdery mildew in our tunnels  and the idea with that is that you apply  the silicon one time or twice a year  and you just leave it in the soil the  plant uptakes it into the leaf and you  don't have to worry beyond that  so we decided to  use a soil applied silicon treatment we  also have a foliar treatment which is  still matrix and then we wanted to use  popular biological uh control so we went  with mill stop and cease and then we  also have trigo which is a conventional  um fungicide  so uh the reason we chose this layout  for this trial um is we were initially  thinking that we would apply  the uh velocinite or the vancil twice in  the season but uh after some further  evaluation we decided we just wanted to  do it early season so that's why the  soil is exposed in the plots uh because  we wanted easy access to mix in the loss  tonight throughout the season  additionally there's wide spacing  between the plants because i come  through here once a week and i rate each  plant for its powdery mildew incidence  so if the leaves are touching each other  or the plants all crossed up it's  difficult to rate for powdery mildews  that's why we chose this four foot  spacing between plants and  we opted with white landscape fabric  just to try to keep the heat down in  here i don't know if you've noticed but  it's much hotter in here than it is  outside it's about  90 degrees in here and it's in the mid  70s outside and also i have this exact  trial replicated in the grow dome and  the grow dome it's probably about 95  degrees right now so it's very warm in  there so powdery mildew as ali and chris  were talking about earlier it is a  fungal pathogen  of a hemp  and basically how powdery mildew works  is that the canadian land on a leaf  and it uh generates a hersudia which is  basically a little root-like structure  that penetrates down into the leaf and  acts as sort of you can think of it  rudimentarily as like a straw and it  sucks the nutrients it needs up into the  host so  and so the idea behind applying silicon  to the uh  to the plants is that silicon is an  important constituent of plant cell  walls it helps to strengthen the cell  wall and so if there is a high level of  silicon in the plant cell in theory you  would think it would be more difficult  for the  powder milder to penetrate into the  plant  so these plants we started them in the  greenhouse  uh middle of may they were allowed to  grow for four weeks we transplanted them  out allowed them to grow for five weeks  vegetatively  and then we came in and inoculated and  sprayed all of our treatments for the  first time the day before we inoculated  we inoculated about four months uh four  weeks ago and we've been rating and  spraying weekly since then  um if you are curious about the rates  that we're using you can use the qr code  outside to pull up our  our  our sheet here yep and so um yeah so  just a little  information on how the different  products work uh mil stop and see  mill stop is  a sodium bicarbonate and that works by  altering the ph of the leaf so fungus  really likes  low ph environments so it looks more  acidic environment and sodium  bicarbonate is basically baking soda it  elevates the ph of the leaf surface and  makes it difficult for the powdery  mildew to grow and that's mixed with a  strain of bacillus which is cease cease  forms a biofilm on the leaf surface it  makes it difficult for the powdered  mildew to establish itself because it  has to compete with another living thing  on the surface of the leaf  and then we have trigo that's a  more conventional  option there it's actually only labeled  for ornamentals so since we're not using  these plants  we're just looking at them it's okay to  use and we chose that because we wanted  to prove that we could effectively  control powdery mildew in the tunnel and  that works by inhibiting the respiratory  pathway of the fungus so that means that  it's more difficult for the fungus to  create energy when it's on leaf surface  so so far we are three weeks into this  project and as you can see we have  plenty of powdery mildew germinating on  our leaves we chose the cultivar white  cbg because it's known to be highly  susceptible to powdery mildew and i  invite you all to walk around and look  and ask questions you'll you'll notice  that there are some differences between  the different treatments this treatment  this so each  row of plants is a replicate  and so this row right here is labeled  very simply so you can see what it is  but if you want to walk around and look  at other plots if you look on that end  of the plots there's a yellow tag yep  did you start on the same time  yes yeah  so yeah i don't know i invite you all to  look at the high tunnel or at the grow  dome the pla some of the plants in there  over eight feet tall so  just check it out um but um  yeah so just feel free to walk around  look at the plants ask me any questions  you might have you can go ahead and you  can notice some differences between the  different treatments there's nothing  concrete right now but i mean obviously  the trifloxi strobe and triangle is  working great everything else there is a  little bit of powder and it'll do here  and there so  a couple of people have asked me two  questions one did you start these from  seed or cuttings before bringing them  out and the other  uh why do you think the plants are such  different heights okay that's a great  question so question is did i start the  plants from seeds or cuttings so this is  all started from seed and as you know  when you start from seed it's a mixed  bag  you are  you hope that your plants are stable but  sometimes they're segregating for  different treats so another question is  why are my plants different heights so  if you stand out back here and look  you'll notice that those plants are  pretty stunted these plants are fairly  stunted these are okay size that is  because a week after we planted uh the  signs of the we hadn't had the science  program correctly at the time and it  just dumped rain and flooded those  outside plots and it took about two  weeks for them to dry out  um so yeah that is from water  flooding stunting um  right there and then within the plots  you might notice some variable height  this population of plants is segregating  for flowering time so uh some of the  plants started flowering about three  weeks ago and some of them just started  flowering this week and a few of them  you'll notice haven't really quite  gotten there yet um so obviously when  the plant begins to grow  uh starts to flower it ceases its um  vegetative growth so that's some of the  variable height within the plots  um any other questions welcome  yes  yeah so the question is uh is there any  anticipated post harvest issues quality  issues with the silica and so we have  taken uh leaf samples from all of our  silica plots and the control plot we're  going to compare what the level of  silica is in the control and in the leaf  of the foliar applied silica and the  soil applied silica and we should be  able to evaluate if it's at a safe level  from that point and also evaluate how  well the plants are able to take up the  silica  and we spray the silica the foliar  silica weekly  oh we spray it actually we start we're  going to continue to spray it throughout  the extent of this study so  well into flowering  yes hi  well what is the contributing factor to  the size difference in between um here  and then  all right this is a great question so  the question is what do i think the  contributing factor is between the size  difference here and in the film and  answer that question is  um so  we have okay if you just look back here  there's a little funny looking arm  situation and that black thing on top is  a par sensor and we also have a power  sensor and grow dome um so one of the  main things i think is that it's much  warmer in the grow dome in here and  obviously the plants are really enjoying  that the second thing is that  uh the par  in this area is there there's more  planted parts plant available radiation  so that's how much radiation is  available for the plant to  photosynthesize the par is higher in  here just by nature of the fact that  this is a clear plastic and the growth  is covered in  white plastic and if you look in there  there's a greater internode length and  usually when the light level is low the  plants will stretch more to  obtain more light so i think it's a  factor of the fact that it's much hotter  in there the plants are very happy with  that also there's greater internode  length because there's less plant  available radiation  any other questions savannah we have a  question yes um a question about the  triangle mainly i know that that's  labels that you as a systemic and you  can go up to 28 days between  applications on that so did you go that  length did you see better results with  triangle and those are any fighting  toxicity great question so the question  is um  trigo which is the  more conventional style fungicide it's  labeled you can spray you can wait  between 28 days to spray the next  ground of that and have we seen  phytotoxicity and what have we done so  we opted to go for a two week interval  with our trigo um and we haven't noticed  any photo toxicity yet we've only  sprayed it twice at this point  um but we will be  noting if there are any effects from  that and it has had excellent control so  far i haven't seen any powdered mildew  on any of our tribal plots at any of the  four locations where this trial is  replicated  that's a great question  i'm not sure yet but  i'm definitely sure that  there will be a link to it on the  cornell hemp website so feel free to  check that out and maybe larry will  tweet about it when it comes out though  our chris  all right any other question  yeah that's a great question so one  thing that i didn't go over that's  really important is that powdery mildew  really enjoys it when it's warm when  there's low  actual like  wetness so it doesn't like a lot of rain  but it likes it when it's very humid um  and so  it does enjoy this environment because  it's warmer than the outdoors and also  because it doesn't rain on the leaves  and because there's high humidity in  here and powdery mildew also really  likes it when there's a strong binaural  fluctuation in the  temperature so when it's cooler at night  and warmer during the day and in here it  does cool off at night time so it enjoys  that aspect of this environment  um  yeah so that's the answer to that  question any other questions  um  [Music]  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

